# Source is text, and has to be diffed as text.
#
# `DataTable.jsx` carried a literal NUL byte in its search separator, which is
# how git classifies a file as binary: `git diff` printed "Bin 9519 -> 14416
# bytes" for the one component every list page in this console renders through,
# `grep -r` reported "binary file matches" instead of the line, and a review UI
# showed the change as an opaque blob. The byte is now written as an escape —
# this is what stops the next one from hiding a diff before anyone notices.
*.js  diff
*.jsx diff
*.css diff
*.md  diff

# Line endings are LF in the tree, whatever a checkout writes.
#
# Without this, an editor or tool on Windows rewrites a file as CRLF and git
# reports every line as changed. Twenty files sat in `git status` for weeks that
# way, from branches whose work had already merged — a one-line edit to
# api-contract.md showed as 11,806 changed lines, and nobody could say which of
# the "modified" files held real work. `core.autocrlf` is a per-clone setting
# and cannot be relied on to be off on the next machine; this is the half the
# repository controls.
#
# It also protects §33. `deploy/olm/*.yaml` is upstream's release vendored byte
# for byte and pinned by SHA-256 in olm_bundle.py, which refuses to load them if
# the bytes change. A checkout that wrote them CRLF would break that hash — the
# OLM install would fail at load with a message about a build that left the
# files out, on a clone where nothing was wrong except the line endings.
#
# `text=auto`, not `text`: git's own binary heuristic still decides, so images
# and fonts are left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf
